# zig-only
# The statusline is clickable end to end: the position chip opens the
# go-to-line prompt, the mode chip toggles the keymap (the vim chip grows
# its glyph), the language chip says what the file is. At 120x40 the
# statusline is row 38; the right lane from the edge is the language chip
# (6 plus its arrow), the workspace (the runner's 41-character temp name
# plus the folder glyph, 44), the clock (7), the idle bell (4), the
# position (14) and the size (6): the position chip spans columns 44–57,
# the language chip 114–119, the mode chip 0–5.
write notes.txt hello world
open notes.txt
expect screen contains "EDIT"
expect screen contains "Ln 1/1 Col 1"
click 50 38
expect screen contains "Go to line"
key esc
click 2 38
expect screen contains "NORMAL"
click 2 38
expect screen contains "EDIT"
click 116 38
expect screen contains "language: txt"
